@page "/docs/cli/blocks"

@* izzy add <block>: where a block goes, --into and --at, recipes, settings, and the .Client project. *@

<ArticlePage Section="cli" Slug="blocks">
    <Lead>
        A block is a ready-made section of a page, such as a hero, pricing or a sign-up form. <Code>izzy add</Code> adds
        the block's component to your app and places it on a page. To browse them, see
        <Anchor Href="blocks">Blocks</Anchor>, where each one has a live preview and its settings.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Add a block</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Run <Code>izzy add</Code> in your app's folder with the block's name. Add its settings as options:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy add hero --layout Split</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            izzy puts the block's component in <Code>Components/Blocks</Code> and adds the block to a page. Most blocks go at
            the end of <Code>Home.razor</Code>. A footer goes in the layout.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Choose where it goes</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            To add the block to another page, set <Code>--into</Code>. To add it at a specific line, set
            <Code>--at</Code> with the file and line number.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Some items are recipes, such as an empty state. A recipe has no usual place on a page, so you always choose
            the line:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy add empty-state --at Components/Pages/Orders.razor:24</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">Settings</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            To list a block's settings, run <Code>izzy describe</Code> with its name, such as <Code>izzy describe hero</Code>.
            The settings have the same names as the starter's options in <Code>dotnet new</Code>, so you can use the same
            spelling in both.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Apps with WebAssembly or Auto pages</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            When your pages are in a <Code>.Client</Code> project, izzy adds blocks there too. Code that reads the request,
            such as the cookie notice's, stays in the server project.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>
